# Line-ending normalization.
#
# This repository's working tree had a pre-existing mix of CRLF and LF line
# endings (inherited from how it was originally checked out), which made
# `git status` and `git diff` report nearly every file as changed even when
# nothing meaningful had changed. This file fixes that going forward.
#
# `text=auto` tells Git to detect text files automatically and normalize
# their line endings to LF inside the repository (the blob Git actually
# stores and diffs), while `eol=lf` also checks them out as LF in the
# working tree, so the same file looks identical whether you're on Windows,
# macOS, or Linux, and `git diff` only ever shows real content changes.
#
# This file changes how *future* commits are normalized. It does not, by
# itself, retroactively rewrite files already on disk. See the root
# README's Known Issues for the one-time cleanup step if you want to fully
# resolve the existing mismatch (git add --renormalize .), which should be
# done as its own commit, separate from any real code change, so the diff
# is easy to review and doesn't get mixed up with actual work.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Common source and config files: explicit for clarity, even though the
# `text=auto` rule above already covers them.
*.py text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.jsx text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Windows batch/PowerShell scripts, if any get added later, should stay CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary files: never touch these, no line-ending conversion, no text diff.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.svg binary
*.pdf binary
*.docx binary
*.xlsx binary
*.pptx binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary
